  [1mB. [38;5;153mlogin[39m owns persistence[0m
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m writes the user into the store itself as a side effect; callers just [38;5;153mawait login(...)[39m and everything
  downstream reads the module.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m fewer steps at the call site, but [38;5;153mlogin[39m now does two jobs — authenticate [3mand[0m mutate global state — so you
  can't test authentication without stubbing storage, and any future non-login path to a session (restore, impersonate,
  test fixture) has to route through a function named [38;5;153mlogin[39m. Rough complexity: trivial.

  [1mC. Observable session with subscriptions[0m
  Everything in A, plus [38;5;153msubscribe(callback)[39m so later forms react to login and logout instead of reading on demand.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the right shape if UI has to update live when the user changes. Today there is one form and no second
  consumer, so the subscription machinery would be built against an imagined requirement. Rough complexity: high.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. It's the same public surface C would have, so adding subscriptions later is additive rather than a
  rewrite — which is exactly why building them now is premature. B's brevity isn't worth welding authentication to
  storage in the one file you'll most want to test.

  I also need one setup decision. This project has no linter, no formatter, no test runner, and no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block — and a
  session module with real branching (storage present or not, stored value malformed, user absent) is the first thing
  here genuinely worth testing. Tooling is cheapest to add before the code exists.
